DIAGNOSTIC: dump the CSIPHY WHILE RUNNING, not on stop

THE MEASUREMENT FLAW IT CORRECTS
--------------------------------
The dump from patch 0079 is done in csiphy_lanes_disable(), that is WHEN THE
SENSOR HAS ALREADY STOPPED EMITTING.

For the ERROR bits it makes no difference: they stay latched and survive. But
not for the STATUS ones: they reflect the end of the transmission, not the
transmission.

And a wrong conclusion was drawn from that. It was observed that the status
register of the CLOCK lane (0x794) read 0x01 both while emitting and while
silent -"it does not flinch"- whereas the data lane ones did change, and that
was taken as an indication that the sensor was not providing a high-speed clock.

But in BOTH cases it was being looked at when there was nothing left to see. It
was not a measurement: it was the instrument looking too late.

WHAT IT DOES
------------
A parameter that dumps the CSIPHY space AT ANY MOMENT from user space. That way
it can be read with the sensor really transmitting, which is the only way to
answer the question that is still alive:

    does the clock lane ever enter high speed?

If with the sensor emitting the clock lane's status stays the same as at rest,
the sensor is not providing a clock and the problem is in the physical link. If
it changes, the clock arrives and the fault is in the sampling of the data
lanes.

It is the first measurement that separates those two things.
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy-3ph-1-0.c	2026-08-17 17:08:57.514907994 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy-3ph-1-0.c	2026-08-17 17:09:22.934201082 +0200
@@ -1060,6 +1060,61 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(termination_pulse,
 	"Write 0x01 to CTRL33 (0x0884), the termination pulse mainline leaves out. 1 = yes.");
 
+/*
+ * DUMP THE PHY WHILE RUNNING, NOT WHEN STOPPING.
+ *
+ * The 0079 dump is done in csiphy_lanes_disable(), that is WHEN THE SENSOR
+ * HAS ALREADY STOPPED TRANSMITTING. The ERROR bits survive latched, but the
+ * STATUS ones -like the clock lane one- reflect the end, not the transmission.
+ *
+ * That is why the clock lane register read the same transmitting as silent: it
+ * was being looked at in both cases when there was nothing left. It is a bug in the
+ * a measurement, not a data point.
+ *
+ * With this it can be dumped AT ANY MOMENT from userspace,
+ * with the sensor really transmitting, which is the only way to see if the
+ * carril de clock llega a entrar en alta speed.
+ */
+static struct csiphy_device *phy_saved;
+
+static void dump_phy(struct csiphy_device *csiphy, const char *when)
+{
+	int a;
+
+	for (a = 0; a < 0x900; a += 32)
+		dev_info(csiphy->camss->dev,
+			 "csiphy%d %s %03x: %08x %08x %08x %08x %08x %08x %08x %08x\n",
+			 csiphy->id, when, a,
+			 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x00),
+			 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x04),
+			 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x08),
+			 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x0c),
+			 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x10),
+			 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x14),
+			 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x18),
+			 readl_relaxed(csiphy->base + a + 0x1c));
+}
+
+static int dump_now_set(const char *val, const struct kernel_param *kp)
+{
+	int ret = param_set_int(val, kp);
+
+	if (!ret && phy_saved)
+		dump_phy(phy_saved, "RUNNING");
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static const struct kernel_param_ops dump_now_ops = {
+	.set = dump_now_set,
+	.get = param_get_int,
+};
+
+static int dump_now;
+module_param_cb(dump_now, &dump_now_ops, &dump_now, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(dump_now,
+	"Write anything to dump the CSIPHY NOW, with the sensor transmitting.");
+
 static int csiphy_dump;
 module_param(csiphy_dump, int, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(csiphy_dump,
@@ -1298,6 +1353,8 @@
 	u8 val, ctrl5;
 	int i;
 
+	phy_saved = csiphy;
+
 	settle_cnt = csiphy_settle_cnt_calc(link_freq, csiphy->timer_clk_rate);
 
 	val = CSIPHY_3PH_CMN_CSI_COMMON_CTRL5_CLK_ENABLE;
